# Ecosystem map implementation checklist

Source proposal: [EcosystemMapProposal.md](EcosystemMapProposal.md). The proposal governs scope and behavior; this checklist governs execution and completion evidence. Completion is recorded per checked item; unchecked items remain pending. Creating this plan does not establish implementation completion.

## Goal and boundaries

Deliver an Ecosystem toggle tab on the full world map, with a localized unique-species dropdown and a translucent suitable-habitat highlight. Show red crosshatching over explored areas with unavailable environmental data; leave confirmed mismatches unmarked. Neither overlay nor controls belong on the minimap.

Discover naturally spawning animal species from all client-local definitions, combining age, sex, and cosmetic variants. Exclude fish, insects, supernatural creatures, and domestic-only variants. Mod-added wildlife participates through the same discovery rules. Supplemental server catalogues are deferred. Local patches remain applicable; server-only catalogue differences are an acknowledged limitation.

Evaluate generated temperature, rainfall, forest, shrubs, and normalized elevation. Suppress surface columns classified as ocean by the native terrain map category. Otherwise ignore water, ground, clearance, light, population caps, player distance, and spawn chance. This is habitat information, not sightings or spawn prediction. Do not add seasonal/weather eligibility or a worldgen/runtime selector.

Receive original server shrub arrays; do not reproduce shrub generation. Capture other inputs through normal client delivery. Omit backfill, remote region loading, terrain generation, and server-side saved-region retrieval to fill old map-only gaps. Reading previously captured local ecosystem records is required and permitted.

Own a separate client-local ecosystem SQLite file per server/world identity. Never open or modify vanilla's map database, connection, tables, or serialized records. Do not infer environmental inputs from rendered map pixels.

## Technical questions to resolve before dependent work

- Target-version asset resolution, regional delivery hooks, full-map identification, explored-coverage access, purge notification, and shutdown ordering require source verification.
- Resolve exact worldgen/runtime rainfall sampling, temperature clamps, elevation sample and surface offset against vanilla behavior; this is technical reconciliation of the approved habitat semantics, not a new product mode.
- Choose concrete paths, types, payload/schema versions, storage identity, and safe array snapshot points after inspecting existing project conventions.
- Determine sample density, queue/batch bounds, and cache limits through representative accuracy and performance measurements rather than speculative fixed budgets.

## Phase 1: Verify integration contracts and establish ownership

Outcome: implementation can use verified target-version entry points without relying on guessed APIs.

- [x] Inspect applicable repository instructions, current feature registration, localization, test, and packaging conventions; record the target game version and relevant source symbols.
- [x] Verify patched client-local animal asset access and reuse of vanilla variant resolution; do not assume client runtime entity spawn conditions are populated.
- [x] Trace generated climate and vegetation sampling in both spawn paths, including reference-height climate, rainfall differences, lower temperature clamps, normalized elevation, and surface offset. Record the reconciled evaluation contract and representative expected values.
- [x] Verify map-layer registration, tab grouping, extra composer lifecycle, full-map versus minimap rendering, and access to explored coverage without querying vanilla's database.
- [x] Verify ordinary region/chunk receipt and already-loaded enumeration; identify safe snapshot timing and server shrub delivery hooks that work while the tab is closed.
- [x] Verify routing through SendMapDataToClient, MapLayerData/MapLayerUpdate, and OnDataFromServer. If a request is necessary, establish player identity without assuming OnDataFromClient supplies it.
- [x] Verify separate database identity/path selection, map-purge notification, and acquisition-stop/flush/close ordering.
- [x] Define directional ownership for species classification, acquisition, shrub delivery, storage, evaluation, and presentation. Keep registration thin and each source file focused; follow project commenting and region conventions.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Record source-backed findings and unresolved blockers beside this checklist; dependent tasks remain blocked when their required hook or data contract is unverified. No scope changes are silently substituted for missing capabilities.

Verification: source inspection, a second review, and an independent `$audit-stage-completion` review support the checked Phase 1 analysis tasks. Linked paths resolve and `git diff --check` passes. No runtime code, build, test, package, visual acceptance, or performance result is claimed.

### Integration contracts (target inspection: 2026-10-18)

These are implementation prerequisites and source findings, not runtime implementation or visual/performance receipts. The proposal remains authoritative. Each numbered entry maps to the corresponding task above; all inherit the proposal's scope, acquisition, ownership, persistence, and lifecycle requirements.

**1. Target and repository conventions.** `modinfo.json` requires game 1.22.0; installed `G:/Vintagestory` has VintagestoryAPI 1.22.0 and VintagestoryLib/VSEssentials/VSSurvivalMod 1.22.7. Inspect these binaries when implementing private adapters; do not infer compatibility with other releases. SHA-256: VSEssentials `A28565C5C9181F8CC84B98A2B7457AB824B8ECB7763714448DA1F7C245AECD6E`; VintagestoryLib `E08F22B493B92FEAF0AAEB79D22437EA0F7EFC38AA7F72A04A47F98BC0E40DF0`. Local API revision `63d33f70018c606fb0f70ead900fe90625fbd7b2`; survival source `849fa8cad9e392368566efc7474e73db6404a145`. Installed code governs disagreements with these source checkouts. Target symbols were inspected through decompilation; temporary files are not build dependencies.

Repository evidence: `VanillaExpanded/src/BucketSourceProtection/BucketSourceProtectionModSystem.cs` demonstrates a feature-owned ModSystem and scoped Harmony lifetime; `src/PlayerWetness/PlayerWetnessSystem.cs` demonstrates client registration/disposal. Add the Ecosystem system under `src/EcosystemMap/`, keep its Start registration thin, register the layer on both sides before `WorldMapManager.OnLvlFinalize`, and read assets after asset finalization. Use registration code `vanillaexpanded:ecosystem` and group `vanillaexpandedecosystem`. Vanilla prepends `maplayer-` to the group before Lang.Get, so provide `maplayer-vanillaexpandedecosystem` in `assets/game/lang/en.json`; an unqualified key belongs to the game language domain. Feature-specific labels belong in `assets/vanillaexpanded/lang/en.json`. Species labels can use explicit `vanillaexpanded:` keys.

`VanillaExpanded.csproj` targets net10.0, references game libraries through `VINTAGE_STORY`, and exposes internals to `VanillaExpanded.Tests`; tests use xUnit/Moq, integration tests reference the test project. `build.ps1` invokes CakeBuild; its default ValidateJson -> Build (clean/publish) -> Package path copies assets/modinfo into Releases and creates the ZIP. Keep the universal mod's `requiredOnServer: false`; missing shrub support affects this overlay's uncached coverage only. Run builds/tests through subagents. Use XML documentation on classes/methods, explanatory comments for nontrivial logic, Public API then Private regions, and focused domain files. No applicable checked-in AGENTS.md was found in this worktree or its ancestors; session instructions govern. No build/test/package claim is made for this documentation-only inspection.

**2. Local assets and variant resolution.** [IAssetManager](../../../../vsapi/Common/Assets/IAssetManager.cs) and [IAssetOrigin](../../../../vsapi/Common/Assets/IAssetOrigin.cs) expose local origins and category enumeration. Important correction to the assumed easy lookup: [AssetCategory.entities](../../../../vsapi/Common/Assets/AssetCategory.cs) is Server-only. Installed `AssetManager.InitAndLoadBaseAssets` / `AddExternalAssets` filter by side; `TryGet` only searches registered assets. Therefore `capi.Assets.GetMany("entities/")` is not a valid baseline acquisition contract. Client runtime `EntityTypeNet` does not restore spawn conditions either.

The viable local source is the installed content origins: use `IAssetOrigin.GetAssets(AssetCategory.entities)` on gameplay-capable origins in the actual manager's load order, preserving location override precedence and copying data into a feature-owned asset view. Do not modify the live asset manager or global category side flags. This supports archive-backed mods as well as directories; do not scan only loose installed files. `AssetManager.GetAssetsDontLoad` supplies the exact origin eligibility/override behavior to follow (later origins overwrite earlier entries in the inspected implementation, despite the interface's priority prose).

Installed `RegistryObjectType.solveByType(JToken, string, OrderedDictionary<string,string>)` is protected static and uses no server context: it applies vanilla wildcard first-match/merge and placeholder substitution. Reuse this operation through one checked adapter on a deep-cloned entity token, using full code and Variant from the client's registered entity identities. `ModRegistryObjectTypeLoader.GatherVariants` and `EntityType.CreateAndPopulate` require server context and must not be run on the client. Decode the resolved spawn-condition object using vanilla JSON defaults and its OnDeserialized climate override; do not use SpawnConditions.Clone, which omits Climate. This avoids inventing a variant Cartesian product or duplicating byType resolution.

The origin path and pure resolver are verified; applying local entity JSON patches uses an isolated patch context. `ModJsonPatchLoader` runs on both sides at order 0.05, yet its default entity-target patches are filtered out on the client by the target's Server category. Its `ApplyPatches("entities/")` can reuse vanilla patch ordering, conditions, dependencies, wildcard targets and operations, but reads `api.Assets`, `api.Side`, `api.World`, `api.ModLoader`, `api.Logger` and writes patched asset bytes. The source-backed adapter contract is a fresh patch-loader instance bound to a feature-owned ICoreAPI view: Assets is the cloned local entity/patch store, Side is Server (the entity definition target), World supplies the synchronized config and logger read by the patcher, and ModLoader/Logger reflect the local installation. Bind its `api` and `worldConfig` fields through a checked adapter, then call `ApplyPatches("entities/")`; do not call AssetsLoaded, which would apply every category. Inspection of the full patcher shows no ICoreServerAPI cast or server-system dependency. Unsupported API operations in this limited view fail closed. Phase 2 must implement and test this boundary, preserve original local origin order, and prove cloned patch targets/bytes never alias live assets. A raw-origin catalogue or ordinary client-patched-assets assumption does not satisfy Phase 2. Do not silently substitute unpatched rules or introduce the deferred server catalogue. This is a verified call boundary, with implementation and patch/isolation receipts required by Phase 2.

**3. Reconciled environmental evaluation.** Sources: installed `GenCreatures.OnChunkColumnGen` / `TrySpawnGroupAt` / `CanSpawnAtConditions`; `ServerSystemEntitySpawner.TrySpawnSomethingAt_offthrad` / `GetSuitableClimateTemperatureRainfall`; `ServerWorldMap.getWorldGenClimateAt` / `AddWorldGenForestShrub`; [Climate.cs](../../../../vsapi/Common/Climate.cs), [SpawnConditions.cs](../../../../vsapi/Common/Entity/SpawnConditions.cs), and [IMapChunk](../../../../vsapi/Common/API/IMapChunk.cs). Surface candidates in both paths start at `WorldGenTerrainHeightMap[z * chunkSize + x] + 1`. Use that generated surface Y for elevation, not rain height, current terrain, or climate-reference Y. Normalize at/below sea level as y/seaLevel and above as 1+(y-seaLevel)/(worldHeight-seaLevel); comparisons include both endpoints. Out-of-world candidates remain invalid.

The catalogue retains definition provenance, but GPU evaluation receives exactly one active set of min/max ranges for the selected species. There is no GPU alternative loop, multi-species buffer or CPU suitability grid. The active set carries one sampling convention. Do not silently union differing definitions; the controls prefer ongoing-spawn definitions, fall back to worldgen, and choose the representative by ordinal entity code within that path. Both inspected conventions use generated maps rather than seasonal weather.

| Component | Worldgen alternative | Ongoing-spawn alternative |
|---|---|---|
| Climate interpolation | Four chunk-corner unpadded climate samples; vanilla BiLerpRgbColor at block centre `(local+0.5)/chunkSize` | ClimateMap.GetUnpaddedColorLerpedForNormalizedPos at candidate integer X/Z divided by regionSize |
| Temperature | Climate.GetScaledAdjustedTemperatureFloat(rawTemp, (int)(seaLevel*0.09)) | Same server conversion with distance `(int)(seaLevel*1.09)-seaLevel` |
| Rain | Interpolated raw rain byte /255f | Climate.GetRainFall(rawRain, (int)(seaLevel*1.09))/255f |
| Forest/shrubs | Four chunk-corner scalar samples, vanilla BiLerp at block centre, /255f | GetUnpaddedColorLerpedForNormalizedPos; forest /255f, shrub low byte /255f |
| Elevation | Generated terrain height +1 | Generated terrain height +1 |

Use each original map's InnerSize and padding; never assume equal forest/shrub/climate resolutions. Worldgen chunk-corner integer selection follows `InnerSize / chunksPerRegion` and vanilla truncation. Use the **server** temperature conversion (lower clamp -20), not the client's -50 variant. Runtime default ClimateValueMode is WorldGenValues; MatchesClimate reads WorldGenTemperature, and forest/shrubs remain generated maps. MinForestOrShrubs uses max(forest, shrubs); comparisons are inclusive. Nondefault locally patched climate modes cannot be interpreted as weather eligibility under the approved semantics; retain generated-value habitat evaluation and document the distinction. No new mode selector or physical spawn checks are introduced.

Representative independent expected values for later tests (uniform regional maps, seaLevel=110, worldHeight=256): raw temperature/rain 128 gives temperature `(128-6)/4.25-20 = 8.705882...` in both paths, worldgen rain `128/255 = 0.5019608...`, ongoing rain `132/255 = 0.5176471...` (integer 9/2 =4). A minRain 0.51 rule therefore distinguishes paths. Raw temperature 0 clamps to -20 server-side, while the client helper yields about -21.41176. Terrain height 109 gives candidate Y110 -> elevation1; terrain110 gives Y111 -> `1+1/146`; Y55 ->0.5. Forest128/shrubs64 gives max128/255, so minForestOrShrubs0.5 passes. Retain each reference-height expression rather than algebraically combining the floating-point casts. The prior CPU boundary receipts do not verify GPU behavior. Phase 5 source review must preserve these expressions; integrated sampling and threshold checks, including GPU float precision, belong to Phase 6.

**4. Full-map UI and explored coverage.** Installed `WorldMapManager.RegisterMapLayer<T>`, `getTabsOrdered`, `OnLvlFinalize`, `GuiDialogWorldMap.ComposeDialog`, `updateMaplayerExtrasState`, `Open`, `GuiElementMap.RenderInteractiveElements`; [OreMapLayer](../../../../vssurvivalmod/Systems/Prospecting/OreMapLayer/OreMapLayer.cs). Layer constructor contract is `(ICoreAPI, IWorldMapManager)`. Use an independent group, initial Active=false, DataSide=Server to participate in server callbacks, RequireChunkLoaded=false. Compose extras with key `worldmap-layer-` + LayerGroupCode; replace/dispose old composer on recomposition and retain selected species in session state. Extras are composed only for EnumDialogType.Dialog and enabled only for active full-map groups. All layers still receive Render on both map elements: explicitly require Active, open dialog and DialogType.Dialog; map open/close notifications alone are insufficient because switching to the HUD can occur without OnMapClosedClient. The element's own dialog reference must identify the rendered surface; gate against the full composer's map element, not solely the manager's current dialog type.

Explored coverage comes from `ChunkMapLayer.loadedMapData` (private ConcurrentDictionary) and public `MultiChunkMapComponent.chunkCoord` / `IsChunkSet(dx,dz)` / `AnyChunkSet`. A checked read-only adapter can snapshot the live per-tile mask on the client thread. Vanilla loads persisted pixel tiles itself on view changes; wait for their in-memory presence before displaying either overlay. Inspect the same loaded components used by the terrain renderer, not LoadedChunks or all visible coordinates (neither proves mapped pixels exist). Honor terrain Active state; absent components fail closed. Each component covers 3x3 chunks, and unset/removal/TTL must revoke coverage. No vanilla database handle, query, pixel inference, or permanent ecosystem exploration index is needed. Overlay samples clip to these chunks and the GUI map scissor. Test coverage changes/recomposition/minimap switching in Phase 6; appearance remains user-run acceptance.

**5. Normal acquisition and original shrubs.** [IEventAPI.MapRegionLoaded](../../../../vsapi/Common/API/IEventAPI.cs) is documented for the server, but installed `SystemNetworkProcess.ProcessInBackground`, packet42, enqueues its client trigger after region UpdateFromPacket and insertion. The acquisition implementation snapshots climate/forest directly at this completed packet42 boundary rather than consuming the deferred event: it can retain the receipt-time purge epoch before the event is queued. Enumerate `ClientWorldMap.MapRegions` through the checked adapter at initialization to include already-loaded neighbouring regions. Clone captured array references and layout together, verify each layout, and never pass game-owned arrays to workers.

For elevation, packet17 calls `ClientMapChunk.UpdateFromPacket` on the network processing thread, then inserts it into MapChunks. There is no equivalent public map-chunk event. The implementation captures at the enclosing `ProcessInBackground` boundary after this update and insertion, retaining packet coordinates and owned heights. Its prefix/finalizer gate protects the entire dictionary lookup/update/insertion, rather than only the inner height-copy call. The same gate covers `SystemUnloadChunks.HandleChunkUnload` and one-time loaded dictionary enumeration; the gate is released before queue waits or worker joins. Use vanilla's map-size-based index conversion for initial tiles. Regional keys reverse the signed-X addition used by MapRegionIndex2D. `ClientWorldMap.UnloadMapRegion` removes entries from the regional ConcurrentDictionary and does not mutate retained map objects. ClientMapChunk.MapRegion returns null: obtain regional association from coordinates. ClientMapRegion.UpdateFromPacket omits ShrubMap. Missing/invalid arrays remain unavailable.

Server `ServerSystemSendChunks.sendAndEnqueueChunks(ConnectedClient)` sends original existing region packets and invokes `ConnectedClient.SetMapRegionSent(long)` for central regions, loaded neighbours and forced ordinary resends. This call has the exact connection instance and normal region identity; a scoped postfix can obtain `ConnectedClient.Player` and queue a shrub delivery obligation keyed by connection+region+generation. Snapshot only `IWorldManagerAPI.GetMapRegion` (loaded-only), with original integer array/Size/TopLeftPadding/BottomRightPadding. Do not use LoadMapRegion or generation. Drain delivery on the server owner thread when the Ecosystem layer and player are ready; queue before layer creation rather than dropping early sends. At startup, inspect existing connections' MapRegionSent sets on that owner thread for relevant already-sent regions. New connection generations reset deduplication; ordinary forced resends check input revision. Successful client purge uses an optional, versioned cache-reset notification: the server's channel handler supplies authenticated player identity, clears only that connection's revision suppression and re-seeds its already-sent set. The next owner tick reads only loaded regions, covering normal sends racing the notification without loading or saved-region retrieval. Missing/unloaded regions remain unavailable. Snapshot data before serialization; never hold region references in queued jobs.

**Receipt/unload verification:** the enclosing packet and unload boundaries are implemented and tested by the Phase 4 receipts below. Headless stress covers actual packet insertion, initial inventory and synchronized dictionary removal, with the voxel-unload body substituted; a separate test covers the real unload exception path. Gates are released before queue waits/worker joins and through finalizers on exceptions. Live gameplay performance remains a Phase 10 gate.

**6. Layer routing and identity.** Installed `SendMapDataToClient` resolves the exact registered layer type to its code, wraps bytes in MapLayerData.ForMapLayer/Data inside MapLayerUpdate.Maplayers, and sends only to a Playing player. Client dispatch indexes MapLayerRegistry and invokes the matching instance's OnDataFromServer. Register the identical code/type on both sides and delay server dispatch until ordinary initialization/playing; queue early obligations. OnDataFromClient(byte[]) omits the sender even though the outer handler has it. Use server-observed normal region sends for initial delivery; no client environmental request is required. Cache-reset notification uses a separate authenticated channel and is sent only when IClientNetworkChannel.Connected confirms server support; it carries no region or player identifier and triggers only the already-sent, loaded-only flow. Any later request must use authenticated outer-handler context or a separate identity-carrying boundary, never a UID inside untrusted payload bytes. Missing server support sends nothing and leaves missing inputs unavailable; the client never requests an unknown server layer code.

Initial shrub payload version1: version, region X/Z, Size, TopLeftPadding, BottomRightPadding, integer data. Validate lengths/checked size multiplication, nonnegative padding, positive InnerSize and supported version before acceptance. Keep record/session generations outside source array contents. Wire/schema versions are independent; exact encoding and size limits belong to Phase 4 verification.

**7. Dedicated storage, purge and shutdown.** [IWorldAccessor.SavegameIdentifier](../../../../vsapi/Common/API/IWorldAccessor.cs) is globally unique; installed ClientMain obtains it from ServerInfo, and ServerMain sends SaveGameData.SavegameIdentifier. This is the game's own world identity contract across singleplayer and multiplayer. Use a filename-safe digest of that identifier under `GamePaths.DataPath/ModData/vanillaexpanded/ecosystem/`, filename `<world-id-digest>.db`, schema version1. Preserve the original identifier and dimensions/seaLevel in own database metadata to reject identity/layout mismatches. Empty/unavailable identity blocks opening storage; never fall back to a shared filename. Coordinates include dimension where applicable and stay separate from identity. Servers hosting the same copied save identity share vanilla's identity semantics; do not claim endpoint isolation beyond this contract.

Use an EcosystemDatabase subclass of [SQLiteDBConnection](../../../../vsapi/Common/IO/SQLiteDBConnection.cs), owning only that new path, overriding table creation and DBTypeCode; add the installed Microsoft.Data.Sqlite reference needed by the override. The base supports an independent connection, pooling disabled, own transactionLock and Close/Dispose; it is not MapDB. Table names `ecosystem_region` and `ecosystem_map_piece`; records carry original per-component layout/version and explicit completeness, not only the simplified proposal diagram. One serialized storage queue owns connection and transactions. Array jobs, pending durable writes and derived jobs have distinct queues/lifetimes.

No public purge event exists. Vanilla `.map purgedb` installs a command delegate in ChunkMapLayer's constructor that calls its private database's Purge. Observe the **command**, not MapDB: obtain `capi.ChatCommands.Get("map").AllSubcommands["purgedb"]` after layer creation, read its ChatCommandImpl.handler through a checked adapter, and wrap it via public HandleWith. Forward arguments and original result; clear ecosystem records/pending/derived state after successful vanilla return, restore the old handler only if still owned during disposal, and leave unrelated commands untouched. A failed vanilla purge must not be reported as successful ecosystem coordination. Do not patch database methods or acquire the mapdb field. Purge advances a generation, stops stale intake/jobs, drops pre-purge writes, clears own tables, and permits subsequent ordinary capture. Loaded vanilla pixels can remain in memory after purge; do not erase or reconstruct them. New ecosystem results may use only post-purge acquisition or retained explicitly valid state; no stale queued writes can recreate cleared records.

WorldMapManager's LeaveWorld handler sets IsShuttingDown, waits up to one second for its own map thread, disposes the dialog, then calls each layer's OnShutDown and Dispose. Its bounded wait is not an ecosystem flush guarantee. Ecosystem OnShutDown must idempotently stop intake, detach receipt hooks/listeners, advance session cancellation, stop and join its own derived workers, drain accepted persistent writes on its own storage owner, close storage, and clear world state. Dispose is an idempotent fallback. Do not rely on ordering among unrelated LeaveWorld subscribers or use a fire-and-forget flush. Map close only cancels presentation work; capture/storage continue. Clear per-connection server obligations on disconnect/shutdown. Concrete worker shutdown/flush/purge receipts belong to Phases 3–4. View membership changes do not revoke source/world validity; explicit presentation close revokes disposable receipts while accepted persistent writes retain their durability contract.

**8. Directional ownership.** Planned source ownership under `src/EcosystemMap/`: Registration/EcosystemMapSystem owns registration and scoped adapter installation; Catalogue owns species discovery and exclusions, the isolated local asset view and resolved rule alternatives; Acquisition owns client receipt snapshots; Transport owns original server shrub delivery and payload validation; Storage owns records/identity/database/serialized durability; Gpu owns raw input preparation, integer textures and GPU comparison resources; Presentation owns EcosystemMapLayer, dropdown, coverage adapter and render resources. Composition injects dependencies; Catalogue/Storage/Gpu input preparation do not depend on map presentation. Transport and Acquisition submit immutable records to Storage; GPU input preparation reads original snapshots; Presentation supplies one active range set and current explored coverage to the shader. Its incremental view membership protects overlapping receipts, queued work and source-keyed GPU resources; each cache protects current visible keys before shrink/admission and evicts off-view entries first. Version-sensitive member access is restricted to named adapters rather than spread through owners. Each file owns one responsibility; all new classes/methods require XML docs and nontrivial methods explain their process. No runtime stubs are necessary to establish these contracts.

**Remaining gates.** The isolated local patch context is implemented and verified by the Phase 2 receipts below. Command-wrapper behavior is implemented and verified by the Phase 3 receipts below. Snapshot synchronization and actual binding/lifecycle coordination are implemented and verified within the Phase 4 receipts below. Phase 5 GPU backend implementation is complete against the reconciled backend contract; its former CPU completion and timing receipts remain withdrawn. Rendering/coverage adapter behavior (Phase 6) and broader gameplay/cache/queue performance measurements (Phase 10) still require their implementation receipts. These are explicit dependent-work gates, not evidence that their runtime behavior already works. No backfill, server catalogue, or base-database access is authorized as a workaround.

## Phase 2: Construct the local species catalogue

Depends on Phase 1 asset and sampling contracts.

Outcome: the client can obtain stable species identities and complete environmental rule alternatives without a server catalogue.

- [x] Discover all naturally spawning animal species from local definitions, including mod-added wildlife, and apply category/metadata exclusions rather than hardcoded supported species or source files.
- [x] Resolve locally patched definitions using the verified vanilla variant mechanism. Preserve source entity identities and environmental limits for each species.
- [x] Combine age, gender, and cosmetics while retaining genuine species distinctions, including deer types and chicken sex encoded in age variants. Exclude all out-of-scope entities.
- [x] Preserve complete alternative rule sets; never widen separate ranges into invented intermediate habitat. Retain applicable worldgen/ongoing-spawn alternatives under the reconciled sampling contract.
- [x] Provide stable identities and reuse vanilla creature-name localization for later dropdown selection; do not add species language entries.
- [x] Add focused catalogue tests for inventory coverage, exclusions, local patches, variant normalization, alternative preservation, and operation without server catalogue messages.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Discovery and exclusion receipts demonstrate one entry per species with traceable applicable rules; deferred catalogue work is not required.


Implementation details: [SpeciesCatalogue.md](SpeciesCatalogue.md). Discovery covers local game and mod wildlife without a supported-species or source-file list. The approved definition metadata attributes.ecosystemMap.speciesGroup merges breed/cosmetic types, with optional nameKey referencing existing translations. Species without grouping metadata remain discoverable. Verification: main/test builds succeeded; 25/25 focused catalogue tests and 2,091/2,091 full regression tests passed with zero skips or failures. The focused tests execute the real vanilla patch/inheritance/byType implementations, automatic unknown-domain discovery, category exclusions, complete installed test-only inventory coverage, resolved handbook grouping, generic metadata merging and the shipped goat normalization asset. Tests use the installed Newtonsoft.Json/Tavis.JsonPatch binaries and native Lib path. A second review and independent completion audit passed against the revised discovery/metadata/native-name contract. No map UI, storage, live visual or performance completion is claimed.

## Phase 3: Implement environmental records and dedicated storage

Depends on Phase 1 storage and lifecycle contracts.

Outcome: environmental snapshots can survive unload and reconnect independently of vanilla terrain pixels.

- [x] Define versioned regional climate/forest/shrub records preserving original array values, size, and padding; define tile elevation and completeness records. Support partial arrivals and explicit unavailable inputs.
- [x] Implement the feature-owned SQLite schema, connection, serialized access, bounded transactions, and off-render-thread disk work. Store regional arrays once per region and tile-specific elevation separately.
- [x] Scope records to verified server/world identity; use coordinates to associate records with displayed tiles in memory, without cross-database access.
- [x] Implement local cache reads, record updates, and explicit version handling. Unsupported records must not become valid samples; do not introduce historical backfill or a legacy migration subsystem.
- [x] Distinguish persistent environmental snapshots/metadata/completeness from disposable evaluation results, prepared tiles, and textures. Establish stop-acquisition, flush-pending-writes, close ordering for normal exit.
- [x] Establish purge handling that intentionally clears own records, pending writes, and derived state; prevent pre-purge pending work from restoring cleared records. Do not modify vanilla storage.
- [x] Add focused storage tests for exact array/layout round trips, partial records, reopen, identity isolation, version handling, normal-exit flush, and purge ordering.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Storage receipts demonstrate independent persistence and lifecycle behavior; review confirms no ecosystem-owned access to vanilla's database file or connection.

Verification and traceability: record/layout and partial/version tasks -> proposal [persistence](EcosystemMapProposal.md#persistence-in-a-dedicated-ecosystem-database) -> immutable regional/tile records, exact codecs and independent component reads; schema/identity/serialization tasks -> Integration contracts 7 and the same proposal section -> dedicated `EcosystemDatabase`, verified identity/geometry and bounded `EnvironmentalStorage` worker; lifetime/purge tasks -> proposal [lifecycle](EcosystemMapProposal.md#performance-and-lifecycle-expectations) and Integration contracts 7 -> epoch barriers, accepted-write drain and command-only purge binding. Current ownership and schema are described in [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md).

A second implementation review and independent `$audit-stage-completion` audit passed. Final installed-game validation: 23/23 focused storage/purge tests and 2,103/2,103 full regression tests, zero failures/skips; main/test build succeeded with existing warnings outside EcosystemMap. Tests cover exact values/layout, partial arrivals, local reopen, world/dimension isolation, incompatible records/schema, shutdown durability/reentry, stale-work purge prevention and untouched vanilla-file sentinel; source review confirms no vanilla database access. `git diff --check` passed. Actual acquisition attachment/shutdown coordination is covered by the Phase 4 receipts below; UI, package/live integration, appearance and measured performance are not established by these storage receipts.
## Phase 4: Capture ordinary world data and deliver original shrubs

Depends on Phases 1 and 3.

Outcome: normal exploration and already-loaded areas populate persistent environmental records even when the Ecosystem tab is closed.

- [x] Capture safe snapshots of normally received climate, forest, and elevation data, including relevant regions/chunks already loaded at initialization. Reject API placeholders and absent arrays as unavailable.
- [x] Read existing server IMapRegion.ShrubMap values and safely snapshot original coordinates, integers, size, padding, and payload version. Do not call shrub generation or resample its output.
- [x] Deliver through the existing map-layer envelope and decode in the Ecosystem layer; validate payload completeness and layout before accepting records.
- [x] Coordinate delivery with ordinary region availability and player receipt; avoid unnecessary unchanged resends while allowing reconnects and missing client records to acquire available data normally.
- [x] Persist incoming components independently of tab visibility and arrival order. Treat missing server support as unavailable shrub coverage unless valid local cached data exists; leave unrelated mod features usable.
- [x] Connect acquisition shutdown and map purge to the storage lifecycle; finish queued persistent writes on normal exit, and prevent late work from leaking into a new world or restoring purged state.
- [x] Add integration checks for exact transport/storage preservation, already-loaded capture, tab-closed capture, delayed shrubs, missing server support, and a region whose climate was forced after shrub generation.
- [x] Verify that old map-only panning initiates no remote retrieval/loading/generation, ordinary revisiting supplies missing inputs, and local cached records remain usable after region unload and reconnect.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Capture/transport/storage evidence shows original server shrub values survive unchanged and all missing-input states remain explicit, with no backfill path.

